{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "07-structural-induction.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Structural induction",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Prove properties of recursive data (lists, trees, expressions) by induction on how they are built, and prove a small program transformation correct.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lesson 5 said Lean's induction on `Nat` is not an axiom: it comes from how `Nat` is built, from `zero` by `succ`. A base case for `zero`, a step for `succ`. Other types are built the same way. A list is either empty, `[]`, or an element in front of a shorter list, `x :: xs`.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Guess the induction principle for lists: what must you prove to know $P$ holds for every list?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!definition] Structural induction\n> A type defined by constructors has an induction principle with a case per constructor, and a hypothesis for each recursive part. For lists: prove $P([\\,])$, and $P(xs) \\to P(x :: xs)$ for every $x$ and $xs$. Natural numbers are the special case `zero` and `succ n`.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Lists",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "def len {α : Type} : List α → Nat\n  | [] => 0\n  | _ :: xs => len xs + 1\n\ntheorem len_append {α : Type} (xs ys : List α) : len (xs ++ ys) = len xs + len ys := by\n  induction xs with\n  | nil => simp [len]\n  | cons x xs ih => simp [len, ih]; omega",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Induction is on `xs`, the list that `++` recurses on: `(x :: xs) ++ ys = x :: (xs ++ ys)`, which is exactly where the hypothesis applies. Induction on `ys` would get stuck, since `++` does nothing with `ys` until `xs` runs out.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Trees",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "inductive Tree where\n  | leaf\n  | node (l : Tree) (v : Nat) (r : Tree)\n\ndef Tree.mirror : Tree → Tree\n  | .leaf => .leaf\n  | .node l v r => .node r.mirror v l.mirror\n\ndef Tree.size : Tree → Nat\n  | .leaf => 0\n  | .node l _ r => l.size + 1 + r.size",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> A `node` is built from two smaller trees. How many induction hypotheses should its case get?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Two, one for each subtree: the recipe needs the property for both parts before it can build it for the whole.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem Tree.mirror_mirror (t : Tree) : t.mirror.mirror = t := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Mirroring twice gives back the tree.",
      "leanSolution": "  induction t with\n  | leaf => rfl\n  | node l v r ihl ihr => simp [Tree.mirror, ihl, ihr]",
      "hints": [
        "`induction t with | leaf => … | node l v r ihl ihr => …`.",
        "In the `node` case, unfold `Tree.mirror` and use both hypotheses: `simp [Tree.mirror, ihl, ihr]`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem Tree.size_mirror (t : Tree) : t.mirror.size = t.size := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Mirroring keeps the size.",
      "leanSolution": "  induction t with\n  | leaf => rfl\n  | node l v r ihl ihr => simp [Tree.mirror, Tree.size, ihl, ihr]; omega",
      "hints": [
        "As before, but `simp` leaves the sizes of the two sides in the other order: `omega` swaps them."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "A simplifier, proved sound",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Expressions are trees too. Here is a tiny language of sums and products, what an expression means, and a simplifier that removes additions of zero:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "inductive Ex where\n  | num (n : Nat)\n  | add (a b : Ex)\n  | mul (a b : Ex)\n\ndef Ex.eval : Ex → Nat\n  | .num n => n\n  | .add a b => a.eval + b.eval\n  | .mul a b => a.eval * b.eval\n\ndef Ex.simp : Ex → Ex\n  | .add a b =>\n    match a.simp, b.simp with\n    | .num 0, b' => b'\n    | a', .num 0 => a'\n    | a', b' => .add a' b'\n  | .mul a b => .mul a.simp b.simp\n  | e => e\n\n#eval (Ex.add (.num 0) (.mul (.num 3) (.add (.num 4) (.num 0)))).simp.eval",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!note] Soundness is a fold\n> A simplifier is **sound** when it never changes what an expression means. Checking examples cannot show that, for the reason lesson 4 gave; structural induction can: if simplifying the parts keeps their values, simplifying the whole does. ChalkMath's engine argues the same way: a rewrite rule proved to keep the value keeps it inside any larger term, by a structural induction, and the dot on a step says whether its rule has such a proof.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem Ex.simp_sound (e : Ex) : e.simp.eval = e.eval := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove the simplifier sound: it keeps the value of every expression.",
      "leanSolution": "  induction e with\n  | num n => rfl\n  | add a b iha ihb =>\n    simp only [Ex.simp]\n    split <;> simp_all [Ex.eval]\n  | mul a b iha ihb => simp [Ex.simp, Ex.eval, iha, ihb]",
      "hints": [
        "Induction on `e`: three cases, and `add` and `mul` each get two hypotheses.",
        "In the `add` case, `simp only [Ex.simp]` exposes the `match`, and `split` gives one goal per branch.",
        "Each branch knows what `a.simp` and `b.simp` are; `simp_all [Ex.eval]` combines that with the hypotheses."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> Every inductive type comes with its own induction: a case per constructor and a hypothesis per recursive part. Lists, trees and syntax trees are proved about the same way as numbers, and \"a transformation keeps the meaning\" is a structural induction.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Every induction so far ran along a relation: \"is one less than\", \"is less than\", \"is a part of\". Which relations can induction run along? First, relations themselves, and what it means to close one up.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
